[{:db/id 17592186133911 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2642af15-1045-5d0f-97c1-3ce034155f08" "question-89fae1b5-1405-51b4-b87d-75b6acbbdc2e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133466 :question/problem "Find $5\\det(A)-\\det(B),$ where \n![](images/3e/3e712748e8fc744fc15cd6d7c1549aaba236b3254d9c65289288133e5daefad4.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has two identical columns, we obtain\n![](images/0a/0a19cbfc3164a365bb02386d16bac01562a36b39de41a4b2909e4397684f2cac.svg)\n\n- Since $B$ is a diagonal matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\det(B) = 3 \\cdot (-4) \\cdot (-2) = 24\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5\\det(A)-\\det(B) = 5 \\cdot 0 - 24 = -24.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133467 :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n![](images/f1/f1906279bd2a08c55aad73b8974edcccfdd3b156c5d471d185e2edc61e538528.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has proportional rows: \n![](images/f7/f7bdc5e767fbd22592a2b9bf8e51517ad6927e7edc495fd7a6ce73b7ae29e4a1.svg)\nAs a result, $|A| = 0.$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a triangular matrix, so \n\n$$\n|B| = (-1) \\cdot 4 \\cdot (-3) = 12 \\neq 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has a zero row: \n![](images/c1/c1211b127ba1daa7863aef3501287f93fda48791ba3a072b98b777bd54ff31d7.svg)\nAs a result, $|C| = 0.$\n\nTherefore, only $|A|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d2486be8-75b8-51f1-882e-08f8a79961f2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133468 :question/problem "![](images/b3/b36961dd90e73504bf46d63827d1ea68292c01a7990f8fa3eb00eee62a4b1813.svg)\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0.$\n\n- $A$ has a zero column\n\n- $A$ has proportional rows\n\n- $A$ has identical columns" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has a zero column: \n![](images/ce/ce6418b20309f4fdf0fdde343eef9fd6fdaa547d40207382c512537c5e509296.svg)\n\n- $A$ has proportional rows: \n![](images/cd/cdfd4bbe379b1e1c68412fa70c67e4ba9e355a329bb8f457ecb17523755f2ea1.svg)\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-2642af15-1045-5d0f-97c1-3ce034155f08" :question/id #uuid "2642af15-1045-5d0f-97c1-3ce034155f08" :question/math-academy-id "q-82285" :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 5 & \\sqrt{3} & 5 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|B|=|\\begin{aligned}6 & \\sqrt{7} & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|C|=|\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn. \n\n- The matrix $A$ has identical columns: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 5 & \\sqrt{3} & 5 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|A|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has identical rows: \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}6 & \\sqrt{7} & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, $|A|,$ $|B|,$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-916484e9-1c80-5d3a-8194-ccf0b63cf5fb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-916484e9-1c80-5d3a-8194-ccf0b63cf5fb" :answer-field/id #uuid "916484e9-1c80-5d3a-8194-ccf0b63cf5fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-545d8b36-38e5-559e-a61d-ce2a27111cbd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e5b9947c-9286-555b-9fb9-6a06456c5033" "answer-6272e38a-2afb-598f-b018-441d73a31140" "answer-246b7875-4b94-5946-ab56-53b0efdaa23b" "answer-545d8b36-38e5-559e-a61d-ce2a27111cbd" "answer-583e2bd2-06b9-53e2-a4b9-5c8a9049a9c1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5b9947c-9286-555b-9fb9-6a06456c5033" :answer/id #uuid "e5b9947c-9286-555b-9fb9-6a06456c5033" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|B|$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6272e38a-2afb-598f-b018-441d73a31140" :answer/id #uuid "6272e38a-2afb-598f-b018-441d73a31140" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-246b7875-4b94-5946-ab56-53b0efdaa23b" :answer/id #uuid "246b7875-4b94-5946-ab56-53b0efdaa23b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$|A|$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-545d8b36-38e5-559e-a61d-ce2a27111cbd" :answer/id #uuid "545d8b36-38e5-559e-a61d-ce2a27111cbd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$|A|,$ $|B|,$ and $|C|$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-583e2bd2-06b9-53e2-a4b9-5c8a9049a9c1" :answer/id #uuid "583e2bd2-06b9-53e2-a4b9-5c8a9049a9c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$|B|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-89fae1b5-1405-51b4-b87d-75b6acbbdc2e" :question/id #uuid "89fae1b5-1405-51b4-b87d-75b6acbbdc2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82192" :question/problem "Find $7det(A)-2det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -0.5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has two identical columns, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ is a triangular matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal: \n\n$$\ndet(B)=3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1\\cdot 2=-6\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7det(A)-2det(B)=7\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-6)=12.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ff922856-e3c4-5b40-8d0e-cd85fae942ae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ff922856-e3c4-5b40-8d0e-cd85fae942ae" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff922856-e3c4-5b40-8d0e-cd85fae942ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b70ba875-07c9-5c3f-85df-154df641f91f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-52a1dbcb-127d-567f-b362-0ca0b1d54e42" "answer-ec20d6e5-b2d8-5e04-b68d-e1fd68f53231" "answer-76cec32a-c27c-5e6c-94e2-bb27825747c5" "answer-b70ba875-07c9-5c3f-85df-154df641f91f" "answer-748fa6b4-08c9-57b1-9c5f-ace131b66c19"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52a1dbcb-127d-567f-b362-0ca0b1d54e42" :answer/id #uuid "52a1dbcb-127d-567f-b362-0ca0b1d54e42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec20d6e5-b2d8-5e04-b68d-e1fd68f53231" :answer/id #uuid "ec20d6e5-b2d8-5e04-b68d-e1fd68f53231" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76cec32a-c27c-5e6c-94e2-bb27825747c5" :answer/id #uuid "76cec32a-c27c-5e6c-94e2-bb27825747c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b70ba875-07c9-5c3f-85df-154df641f91f" :answer/id #uuid "b70ba875-07c9-5c3f-85df-154df641f91f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-748fa6b4-08c9-57b1-9c5f-ace131b66c19" :answer/id #uuid "748fa6b4-08c9-57b1-9c5f-ace131b66c19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d2486be8-75b8-51f1-882e-08f8a79961f2" :question/id #uuid "d2486be8-75b8-51f1-882e-08f8a79961f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42463" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\n- $A$ has identical rows\n- $A$ has proportional columns\n- $A$ has a zero row" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have proportional columns.\n\n- $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3924ef22-234b-5f76-ad06-7ddc5f55eff3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3924ef22-234b-5f76-ad06-7ddc5f55eff3" :answer-field/id #uuid "3924ef22-234b-5f76-ad06-7ddc5f55eff3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2c504700-553a-587d-937f-efdc724865fe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d56a853-a4e2-5868-a11b-a8fda7f31398" "answer-462e1c30-a350-5b46-a5e5-cd8202fb5f1a" "answer-7c57cc06-ff92-5adf-9092-769447c60c32" "answer-19fc08ca-72fe-570b-a146-ccf5baf503d8" "answer-2c504700-553a-587d-937f-efdc724865fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d56a853-a4e2-5868-a11b-a8fda7f31398" :answer/id #uuid "3d56a853-a4e2-5868-a11b-a8fda7f31398" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-462e1c30-a350-5b46-a5e5-cd8202fb5f1a" :answer/id #uuid "462e1c30-a350-5b46-a5e5-cd8202fb5f1a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c57cc06-ff92-5adf-9092-769447c60c32" :answer/id #uuid "7c57cc06-ff92-5adf-9092-769447c60c32" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19fc08ca-72fe-570b-a146-ccf5baf503d8" :answer/id #uuid "19fc08ca-72fe-570b-a146-ccf5baf503d8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c504700-553a-587d-937f-efdc724865fe" :answer/id #uuid "2c504700-553a-587d-937f-efdc724865fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135151 :tutorial/content "If an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ has an entire row or column of zeros, then its determinant is equal to zero.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A$ given below:\n\n![](images/6b/6bb8b64f7ed8730e641cdf88fc6fe7add40bc15061095c6b1f57c1b764b28feb.svg)\n\nThe second row consists of all zeros, and the asterisks ($\\ast$) may represent any number.\n\nLet's compute the determinant using the Laplace expansion across the second row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)^{2+1}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}|+(-1)^{2+2}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}|+(-1)^{2+3}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+0+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that it doesn't matter what numbers are represented by the asterisks ($\\ast$). Each cofactor is multiplied by zero, so the final result must be zero." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135152 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose a matrix has no zero rows but it has two identical rows:\n\n![](images/74/7477781b618ec384b1638d01707acf545e7d9882ffca5bbc0efde4e224b04308.svg)\n\nWe can subtract the $1$st row from the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this operation does not change the determinant, we get $\\det(A) = \\det(B).$ Furthermore, as $B$ contains a zero row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = \\det(B) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can use this method with columns too. Therefore, we have the following property:\n\n*If a square matrix has at least two identical rows/columns, its determinant equals zero.*" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135153 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose a matrix has no zero rows and no identical rows but it has two proportional rows:\n\n![](images/81/8181d36ff1378310744b0d74f42f2934eb1e41053f45ecfeacf0c813abe994df.svg)\n\nWe can add three times the $1$st row to the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this operation does not change the determinant, we get $\\det(A) = \\det(B).$ Furthermore, as $B$ contains a zero row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = \\det(B) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can use this method with columns too. Therefore, we have the following property:\n\n*If a square matrix has at least two proportional rows/columns, its determinant equals zero.*" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135154 :tutorial/content "In conclusion, if an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ satisfies any one of the following conditions, then $\\det(A)=0.$\n\n- $A$ has a zero row/column\n\n- $A$ has two identical rows/columns\n\n- $A$ has two proportional rows/columns\n\n- $A$ has a row/column that is a linear combination of other rows/columns\n\nNotice that condition IV encompasses each of the conditions I, II, and III. If we have a zero row/column, two identical rows/columns, or proportional rows/columns, then the rows/columns form a linearly dependent set of vectors. \nIn turn, this means that one of the rows/columns is a linear combination of other rows/columns.\n\n**Watch out!** While all of the above conditions are *sufficient* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0,$ only condition IV is *necessary* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0.$ \nIn other words, if we have a matrix $A$ with $\\det(A)=0,$ then conditions I, II, and III may or may not be satisfied, while condition IV *must* be satisfied. \n\nSo, we have the following theorem:\n\n*$\\det(A)=0$ if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nFinally, note also that in the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix *only*, statements III and IV mean the same thing." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135155 :tutorial/content "Geometrically, the absolute value of an $n \\times n$ determinant can be interpreted as the volume of the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the columns of the determinant. \n\nFor example:\n\n- Suppose the columns of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ are ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}}, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{b}},$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{c}}.$\n![](images/6e/6e046341b7a1b8f6760797f7db89a7e021a6ace6b5cdf28bd5217998cb450837.svg)\nThe $3$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the columns of $A$ is depicted below:\n\n![](images/25/2597b48a010607fca71c551dbce8e9e2bbeda47d0892a582347ae1cdf19f1bf6.png)\n\nThe volume $V$ of this parallelepiped equals the absolute value of the determinant of $A{:}$\n\n$$\nV = \\left|\\det(A)\\right|\n$$\n\n- Now suppose the columns of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ are ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{p}}$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}}.$\n![](images/f5/f593c123eb081da0399e74c665325774758b48c5f086a7475b959905e1de2763.svg)\nThe $2$-dimensional parallelepiped (i.e., the parallelogram) spanned by vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/4a/4af1971404b188df75bb3d62d47ac95340c5e1791066e5358370d743230baa45.png)\n\nThe two-dimensional volume $V$ (i.e., the area) of this parallelepiped equals the absolute value of the determinant of $B{:}$\n\n$$\nV = \\left|\\det(B)\\right|\n$$\n\n- In general, the absolute value of the determinant of an $n\\times n$ matrix $M$ gives the $n$-dimensional volume of the parallelepiped spanned by the columns of $M.$\n\nIn this lesson, we studied the following property:\n\n*$\\det(A) = 0$ if and only if one of the columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other columns of $A$.*\n\nWhat does this property mean in geometric terms?\n\nThe component $\\det(A) = 0$ implies that the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped formed by the columns of $A$ has zero volume. Conversely, when one of the columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other columns, it indicates that the corresponding parallelepiped possesses at most $n-1$ distinct dimensions. \n\nConsider the following examples:\n\n- In the case of a $3$-dimensional parallelepiped, if the vector $\\mathbf{c}$ is a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$, then $\\mathbf{c}$ lies within the plane containing $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$. In other words, the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$, $\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\mathbf{c}$ are coplanar. Consequently, we obtain a \"flat\" ($2$-dimensional) figure with a volume of $0$.\n\n- For the parallelogram example, if the vector $\\mathbf{q}$ is a linear combination of the vector $\\mathbf{p}$ (i.e., $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are proportional), then $\\mathbf{q}$ resides on the line containing vector $\\mathbf{p}$. In other words, the vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are collinear. This results in a $1$-dimensional object with a $2$-dimensional volume (i.e., area) of $0$." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134042 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
